    "perplexity": {
      "description": "Released in 1981, Nick Mason’s Fictitious Sports is the Pink Floyd drummer’s first solo album, but it is really a Carla Bley project in Mason’s name: Bley wrote all the material and shaped the album’s off-kilter jazz-rock identity, while Mason’s drums and co-production helped give it a sharper, more rock-oriented edge. The record blends whimsical, theatrical songwriting with nimble ensemble playing, and Robert Wyatt’s lead vocals add a sly, alienated character that suits the music’s dry humor and eccentric swing. Its mix of jazz, rock, and progressive textures makes it one of the more unusual albums associated with a major rock figure from the era.\n\nThe album has long been noted for its low-key but distinctive place in Mason’s catalog, standing apart from Pink Floyd’s sound and from most conventional solo debuts. Recorded in 1979 and issued on Harvest in the UK, it has often been praised for its inventive arrangements and unusual charm, even by listeners who approach it expecting a straightforward rock record. Its significance lies less in displaying Mason as a solo auteur than in capturing a rare, fruitful crossover between British art-rock sensibility and Carla Bley’s sharp, idiosyncratic jazz composition.",
